package com.gielinordeeds;

import javax.annotation.Nullable;
import net.runelite.api.coords.WorldPoint;

/**
 * The rules of Deed Locked: a challenge run where unsurveyed Gielinor is
 * blacked out and you spend what you earn opening more of it.
 *
 * <h2>Why a deed and not a region</h2>
 *
 * A deed is 8x8 tiles, smaller than most banks. The objection to locking at
 * that scale is that a run confined to 64 tiles cannot train, so cannot earn
 * charges, so never unlocks anything.
 *
 * The veil is not a wall. Nothing here blocks movement and the menu filter is
 * off by default, so a fresh account on one deed still has all of Lumbridge to
 * train in. A deed bounds what you can see and what you own; the XP earned out
 * in the dark is what buys the next one.
 *
 * Two rules make the small unit workable:
 *
 *   the opening grant   you own the deed you stand on and hold GRANT_CHARGES
 *                       survey charges. Nothing else is surveyed, and which
 *                       direction to spend them in is yours.
 *   frontier surveying  you may survey any deed touching one you own, so the
 *                       map opens outward from your estate rather than
 *                       requiring you to walk onto ground you cannot see.
 *   outposts            far from everything you hold, you may found an outpost
 *                       on the deed you are standing on, at a premium in
 *                       charges. See {@link #outpostStatus}.
 *
 * <h2>Why the frontier is owned land, not surveyed land</h2>
 *
 * Charges come from XP and money comes from rent. If a survey alone moved the
 * frontier, expansion would need only charges and money would buy nothing you
 * could not already reach. Anchoring it to ownership makes a charge open the
 * ring around what you hold and a purchase move that ring, so neither currency
 * can advance alone.
 *
 * <h2>Why an outpost as well</h2>
 *
 * Gielinor is not one walkable continent and the frontier rule quietly assumed
 * it was. Corsair Cove is reached by ship, so nothing there ever touches land
 * you own: measured against the shipped survey, opening it from Rimmington
 * costs 48 water deeds straight across the sea, or 109 round the coast, and
 * every one of them pays no rent while making all later land dearer. The water
 * tier exists to cross rivers and causeways, and it does that well -- Musa
 * Point is six deeds off Rimmington. An ocean is not a river, and a rule with
 * no legal move in it is a dead end rather than a challenge.
 *
 * So: stand on ground far from everything you hold and you may found an
 * outpost there, paying {@link #OUTPOST_CHARGES} instead of one. Arriving by
 * ship you are stood on a deck rather than on ground, so the deed is read from
 * beneath you by {@link #deedUnder} -- and since every dock moors over water,
 * founding one means walking ashore first. It buys reach
 * and nothing else. The outpost is surveyed, not owned, so it still has to be
 * bought before anything around it can be surveyed -- the two currencies stay
 * locked together exactly as on the frontier, and an outpost is a worse deal
 * per deed than ordinary expansion. Getting there is the qualification, and
 * the game gates that itself with quests, fares and ships.
 *
 * <h2>Enforcement is honour-system</h2>
 *
 * Nothing here blocks movement. The veil hides ground and the menu filter can
 * refuse clicks, but a player who wants to walk out still can.
 */
public final class DeedLock
{
	/**
	 * Survey charges a run begins with.
	 *
	 * Three, and the ground they open is the player's choice. An earlier
	 * version surveyed three neighbours FOR them, which is the same amount of
	 * map and none of the decision: the run opened with a fact rather than a
	 * question. Handing over the charges instead means the first thing that
	 * happens in a locked run is somebody deciding which way to look.
	 *
	 * Before that it was a 5x5 block of 25 deeds -- a small town given away
	 * before the player had done anything, so the opening was survived rather
	 * than played and the first real choice arrived an hour in.
	 */
	public static final int GRANT_CHARGES = 3;

	/** How far the opening looks for ground it can give you. */
	private static final int GRANT_SEARCH = 1;

	/**
	 * Charges to found an outpost, against one for an ordinary survey.
	 *
	 * Five is roughly forty minutes against the hourly cap, so a new region is
	 * an expedition rather than an impulse -- and it is deliberately a bad deal
	 * per deed. Ordinary expansion opens a deed for one charge and a purchase;
	 * an outpost opens one deed for five and leaves you owning nothing around
	 * it. What it buys is somewhere the frontier could never have reached.
	 */
	public static final int OUTPOST_CHARGES = 5;

	/**
	 * How far from your own land an outpost has to be, in deeds.
	 *
	 * Twelve deeds is 96 tiles -- well out of sight of anything you hold, and
	 * far enough that this can never be used to hop your own frontier and
	 * dodge the purchase that is supposed to move it.
	 *
	 * The threshold is measured from YOUR land, not from the mainland, and that
	 * is the whole of its logic: it asks whether you have a supply line, not
	 * whether there is water in the way. An estate on the facing shore is
	 * inside it and buys the crossing; an estate a continent away is outside it
	 * and plants a flag.
	 *
	 * That lands where it should, measured against the shipped survey. Coast to
	 * coast, Karamja is 2 deeds off Kandarin, Entrana 3, Crandor 5, Miscellania
	 * 9 -- every one a hop the water tier already handles, and every one still
	 * inside the threshold for a player who has expanded to meet it: Musa Point
	 * is 6 deeds off Rimmington and Karamja's west tip 7 off Port Khazard. From
	 * a Lumbridge estate those same places are 38 and 66 deeds away, and there
	 * is no supply line to speak of. Kourend is 31 deeds off the mainland and
	 * 201 from Lumbridge; Corsair Cove is 48 by sea from Rimmington.
	 */
	public static final int OUTPOST_MIN_DEEDS = 12;

	/**
	 * Why the deed underfoot can or cannot be founded on.
	 *
	 * A boolean would have been enough to run the rule and useless to explain
	 * it, and a player standing on unsurveyable ground with charges in hand and
	 * a dead button is owed a reason. The panel prints one per case.
	 */
	public enum Outpost
	{
		/** Found it: far from everything you hold, and dry land. */
		ALLOWED,
		/** Ordinary frontier survey applies -- no outpost needed. */
		ON_FRONTIER,
		/** Within {@link #OUTPOST_MIN_DEEDS} of land you own. Buy across. */
		TOO_CLOSE,
		/** Open water, or ground the survey will not sell at all. */
		NOT_LAND,
		/** Already surveyed. */
		KNOWN,
		/** Nothing owned yet, so there is nothing to be far from. */
		NO_ESTATE
	}

	/**
	 * Deeds from here to the nearest one you own, or MAX_VALUE if you own none.
	 *
	 * Chebyshev, so it counts in the same steps the frontier walks in: a
	 * diagonal neighbour is one deed away, the same way it is one survey away.
	 */
	public static int deedsToEstate(@Nullable ParcelGrid grid,
		@Nullable Estate estate, @Nullable Parcel p)
	{
		if (grid == null || estate == null || p == null)
		{
			return Integer.MAX_VALUE;
		}
		int best = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
		for (String pid : estate.getOwned().keySet())
		{
			Parcel q = grid.byPid(pid);
			if (q == null)
			{
				continue;
			}
			int d = Math.max(Math.abs(q.getPx() - p.getPx()),
				Math.abs(q.getPy() - p.getPy()));
			if (d < best)
			{
				best = d;
				if (best == 0)
				{
					break;
				}
			}
		}
		return best;
	}

	/**
	 * The deed a position stands over, storeys included.
	 *
	 * The plane is dropped first, the same thing {@link #isVeiled} does and for
	 * the same reason: a deed is a piece of ground and what is built over it
	 * belongs to it. So a first floor, a ship's deck and a rooftop all answer
	 * with the ground underneath.
	 *
	 * <h2>Why it does not go looking for a shore</h2>
	 *
	 * It used to. Every dock in the game is moored over a water deed, so the
	 * deed under a boat is never land, and the first version reached for the
	 * nearest dry deed within three so that an outpost could be founded from
	 * the deck.
	 *
	 * Nearest is not the same as reachable, and Corsair Cove is where that
	 * shows. The deed at the head of the pier you are tied to is three deeds
	 * north; a scrap of shore across open water is two deeds north-west, and
	 * wins on distance. A run that founded there spent five charges on ground
	 * it could not walk to, and was no closer to the cove than before.
	 *
	 * Doing it properly means asking what is walkable rather than what is near,
	 * and that answer is not available from a deck: a gangplank moves you
	 * between planes through an object, which no collision map models as a
	 * step. So the guess is gone rather than refined.
	 *
	 * Nothing is lost by that, because the reason it existed has itself been
	 * fixed. It was written when "lock me to my land" could strand a run on the
	 * boat it arrived on, so founding from the deck was the only move left.
	 * That filter now stands down off your own land, so you can simply walk
	 * ashore -- and the deed under your feet when you get there is unambiguous
	 * in a way that no guess from the water can be.
	 *
	 * Null when there is no ground here at all: a cave, an instance, or
	 * anywhere the survey never covered.
	 */
	@Nullable
	public static Parcel deedUnder(@Nullable ParcelGrid grid, @Nullable WorldPoint wp)
	{
		WorldPoint ground = ParcelGrid.groundOf(wp);
		return grid != null && grid.isSurveyable(ground) ? grid.at(ground) : null;
	}

	/**
	 * Whether an outpost may be founded on this deed, and if not, why not.
	 *
	 * Asks nothing about where the player is standing. That the deed is under
	 * your feet is a separate condition the plugin enforces, because it is what
	 * makes the journey the qualification -- this answers only whether the
	 * ground itself is eligible.
	 */
	public static Outpost outpostStatus(@Nullable ParcelGrid grid,
		@Nullable Estate estate, @Nullable Parcel p)
	{
		if (grid == null || estate == null || p == null)
		{
			return Outpost.NOT_LAND;
		}
		int idx = grid.indexOf(p);
		if (idx < 0)
		{
			return Outpost.NOT_LAND;
		}
		if (estate.hasSurveyed(idx))
		{
			return Outpost.KNOWN;
		}
		// Checked before anything else, so a deed that can be had for one
		// charge is never offered at five.
		if (onFrontier(grid, estate, p))
		{
			return Outpost.ON_FRONTIER;
		}
		if (estate.getOwned().isEmpty())
		{
			return Outpost.NO_ESTATE;
		}
		// Land, not merely claimable. An outpost on open water would pay no
		// rent and could only ever be a way of hopping the frontier cheaply.
		if (!p.isLand())
		{
			return Outpost.NOT_LAND;
		}
		return deedsToEstate(grid, estate, p) < OUTPOST_MIN_DEEDS
			? Outpost.TOO_CLOSE : Outpost.ALLOWED;
	}

	private DeedLock()
	{
	}

	/**
	 * True when this deed may be surveyed from where the estate currently
	 * stands: it is unsurveyed, and it touches a deed you OWN.
	 *
	 * Touching one you have merely surveyed is not enough -- see the class
	 * note. Surveying is what lets you see a deed and learn its price; buying
	 * it is what lets you survey past it.
	 *
	 * Diagonals count. A frontier that only opened along the compass would
	 * make diagonal expansion cost two deeds instead of one, which is a tax on
	 * nothing in particular.
	 */
	public static boolean onFrontier(ParcelGrid grid, Estate estate, @Nullable Parcel p)
	{
		if (grid == null || estate == null || p == null)
		{
			return false;
		}
		int idx = grid.indexOf(p);
		if (idx < 0 || estate.hasSurveyed(idx))
		{
			return false;
		}
		for (int dx = -1; dx <= 1; dx++)
		{
			for (int dy = -1; dy <= 1; dy++)
			{
				if (dx == 0 && dy == 0)
				{
					continue;
				}
				Parcel n = grid.at(p.getPx() + dx, p.getPy() + dy);
				if (n != null && estate.owns(n.getPid()))
				{
					return true;
				}
			}
		}
		return false;
	}

	/**
	 * Start the run, once, wherever it begins.
	 *
	 * You own the deed under your feet, recorded as paid 0, and hold
	 * {@link #GRANT_CHARGES} survey charges. Nothing else is surveyed.
	 *
	 * At minute zero the frontier is the ring around you, since every
	 * neighbour is unsurveyed and touches land you own. Charges reveal those
	 * and then stop: nothing further out touches anything of yours, so the map
	 * goes no further until you buy.
	 *
	 * The deed given is the one you are standing on, or the nearest land if you
	 * are on water. If there is no land in reach nothing is granted at all, so
	 * the run begins properly once you stand somewhere it can.
	 *
	 * Returns 1 when a run begins here, or 0 if the estate already holds
	 * ground, which is what stops it re-firing every time the plugin loads.
	 */
	public static int grantStart(ParcelGrid grid, Estate estate, @Nullable Parcel here)
	{
		if (grid == null || estate == null || here == null || estate.surveyedCount() > 0)
		{
			return 0;
		}

		// Found before anything is written, so a start with nowhere to stand
		// leaves the estate untouched rather than half-begun.
		Parcel deed = nearestLand(grid, here, null);
		if (deed == null)
		{
			return 0;
		}

		estate.markSurveyed(grid.indexOf(deed));
		estate.getOwned().put(deed.getPid(), 0);
		estate.getCharges().grant(GRANT_CHARGES);
		return 1;
	}

	/**
	 * The closest land deed to {@code from} that the estate has not seen.
	 *
	 * Pass a null estate to ignore what has been surveyed, which is how the
	 * very first deed is chosen. Ties break on scan order, which spreads the
	 * opening's neighbours across compass directions rather than stacking them
	 * along one axis -- the four orthogonal neighbours all sit at distance 1
	 * and are visited before any diagonal.
	 */
	@Nullable
	private static Parcel nearestLand(ParcelGrid grid, Parcel from,
		@Nullable Estate estate)
	{
		Parcel best = null;
		int nearest = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
		for (int dx = -GRANT_SEARCH; dx <= GRANT_SEARCH; dx++)
		{
			for (int dy = -GRANT_SEARCH; dy <= GRANT_SEARCH; dy++)
			{
				Parcel q = grid.at(from.getPx() + dx, from.getPy() + dy);
				int idx = grid.indexOf(q);
				// Land, not merely claimable. Open water is buyable now, and a
				// run granted a water deed would own something paying no rent,
				// so it could never afford a second one.
				if (q == null || idx < 0 || !q.isLand())
				{
					continue;
				}
				if (estate != null && estate.hasSurveyed(idx))
				{
					continue;
				}
				int distance = dx * dx + dy * dy;
				if (distance < nearest)
				{
					nearest = distance;
					best = q;
				}
			}
		}
		return best;
	}

	/**
	 * Whether the veil covers this ground.
	 *
	 * The single answer for the whole mode: the veil, the menu filter and the
	 * trespass warning all ask it, so all three agree about the same ground.
	 *
	 * Upper floors belong to the deed underneath them, so the plane is dropped
	 * before the lookup and a building straddling the boundary is half veiled
	 * on every storey.
	 *
	 * Ground the survey never covered is never veiled. Caves, dungeons and
	 * instances lie outside the grid's bounds, and there is no deed there to be
	 * off.
	 */
	public static boolean isVeiled(@Nullable ParcelGrid grid, @Nullable Estate estate,
		@Nullable WorldPoint wp)
	{
		WorldPoint ground = ParcelGrid.groundOf(wp);
		if (grid == null || estate == null || ground == null
			|| !grid.isSurveyable(ground))
		{
			return false;
		}
		int idx = grid.indexOf(grid.at(ground));
		return idx >= 0 && !estate.hasSurveyed(idx);
	}
}

